Step 1: Check the Grafana labels and existing service

Run:

kubectl get svc -n monitoring | grep grafana

Then:

kubectl get svc grafana -n monitoring -o yaml

Also check the deployment labels:

kubectl get deployment grafana -n monitoring --show-labels

From your earlier logs, your Grafana pod is:

grafana-746cbdcf77-4mkwh

So first confirm the exact labels before creating the new service.

Step 2: Create a separate Grafana LoadBalancer service

Create:

cat > grafana-lb.yaml <<'EOF'
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: grafana-external
  namespace: monitoring
  annotations:
    service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-type: "external"
    service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-nlb-target-type: "ip"
    service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-scheme: "internet-facing"
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer
  selector:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: grafana
  ports:
    - name: http
      protocol: TCP
      port: 80
      targetPort: 3000
EOF

Apply it:

kubectl apply -f grafana-lb.yaml

This configuration requests an internet-facing NLB and routes traffic directly to pod IPs.

Step 3: Check whether the Load Balancer is created

Run:

kubectl get svc grafana-external -n monitoring -w

Initially you may see:

EXTERNAL-IP   <pending>

Wait until AWS creates the NLB.

You should eventually see something similar to:

NAME               TYPE           CLUSTER-IP       EXTERNAL-IP
grafana-external   LoadBalancer   172.xx.xx.xx     xxxxx.elb.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com
Step 4: Get the Grafana URL

Run:

kubectl get svc grafana-external -n monitoring

Or directly:

kubectl get svc grafana-external \
  -n monitoring \
  -o jsonpath='{.status.loadBalancer.ingress[0].hostname}'

Then access:

http://YOUR-GRAFANA-NLB-DNS

For example:

http://xxxxx.elb.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com
